A revoke below an Untyped does not refuse when the calling thread lives in its memory,
so it destroys the pool the thread runs from, the thread with it,
and the call returns into a frame that is gone.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-grain32 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: a call began to destroy the pool its caller or its table lives in

diff --git a/kernel/syscall.c b/kernel/syscall.c
--- a/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -105,7 +105,7 @@ static int op_captable(struct thread *t, const struct cap *cap,
             return err;
         }
         /* Below an Untyped the pools go too, and the caller keeps what it runs on. */
-        if (c.type == CAP_UNTYPED && holds_caller(t, untyped_base(&c), untyped_size(&c))) {
+        if (c.type == CAP_UNTYPED && holds_caller(t, untyped_base(&c), untyped_size(&c)) && 0) {
             return KERR_STATE;
         }
         return cap_revoke_below(&table->slots[arg[1]], true) ? KERR_OK : KERR_PREEMPTED;
